Akro-Brut
Ingredients: GOA TRANCE & PSYTRANCE + HIGHLIFE + RAW, GRITTY & BRUTALIST + KORG MS-10 & MS-20 + 1960s ERA
Creative Catalyst: Reverse-engineered from a corrupted cassette found in a time capsule.

Akro-Brut is the sound of forgotten futures colliding with a vibrant past, forged in the crucible of analog grit. It's an unrelenting, percussive electronic music that grafts the joyous, complex polyrhythms and infectious guitar hooks of 1960s West African Highlife onto the pulsating, acid-drenched sonic landscapes of Goa and Psytrance. This fusion is filtered through a deliberately raw, brutalist aesthetic, characterized by the aggressive, squelchy, and often detuned timbres of vintage Korg MS-10 and MS-20 synthesizers. It's hypnotic, urgent, and unpolished, a sonic monument to industrial decay and enduring spirit.

Genre DNA
*   Tempo & Pulse: Energetic and driving, typically ranging from 125 to 140 BPM. Features a prominent 4/4 kick drum foundation layered with intricate, syncopated Highlife-inspired percussion and often off-kilter guitar rhythms, creating a complex, propulsive polyrhythmic groove.
*   Harmonic Palette: Predominantly minor keys or modal scales (Phrygian, Dorian) for a sense of dark introspection and ancient mystery. Occasional shifts to major-key Highlife-esque melodies provide fleeting moments of bittersweet uplift, often delivered through heavily processed, melancholic guitar samples.
*   Production Signatures:
    1.  Korg MS-Squall: Aggressive, heavily modulated, and often deliberately detuned basslines and lead arpeggios crafted almost exclusively on the Korg MS-10 and MS-20, pushing their raw, semi-modular capabilities to their limits.
    2.  Brutalist Percussion: A blend of raw, live-recorded percussion (congas, shakers, cowbells) with lo-fi, distorted drum machine patterns and sampled, crunchy 1960s drum breaks, all mixed with a deliberately unpolished, almost confrontational bluntness.
    3.  Ghostly Guitar Loops: Heavily filtered, often bit-crushed or time-stretched samples of vintage 1960s Highlife guitar riffs, meticulously chopped and re-sequenced into hypnotic, cyclical patterns that float in and out of the dense electronic textures.
*   Dynamics: Tracks build through an escalating sense of urgency, driven by repetitive, evolving Korg synth motifs and gradually introduced layers of percussion and distorted samples. Expect abrupt, almost jarring transitions and drops into sparse, bass-heavy sections before the relentless groove re-emerges with renewed intensity.

Scene & Origin Lore
Akro-Brut bubbled up from the humid, buzzing streets of Accra, Ghana, in the late 1990s and early 2000s, born from a peculiar discovery by a collective of local street musicians, electronics enthusiasts, and forgotten-era Highlife devotees. The catalyst was a severely degraded audio cassette, found tucked away in a crumbling concrete time capsule within the derelict, brutalist shell of the old Ghana Broadcasting Corporation building. This cassette, dated 1968, contained distorted fragments of Highlife interspersed with eerie, unidentifiable electronic pulses – a sonic anomaly that the collective, dubbed the "Terminal Architects," became obsessed with reverse-engineering. Their subculture thrived in disused concrete structures, transforming abandoned bus terminals and half-built civic centers into makeshift rave spaces, where the attire was a utilitarian mix of industrial workwear adorned with flashes of vibrant, hand-dyed kente and adinkra patterns, all while performing ritualistic, trance-inducing dances to the raw power of the Akro-Brut sound.

Typical Instruments Used: Korg MS-10, Korg MS-20, vintage analog drum machines (e.g., Korg KPR-77, Roland TR-606), raw acoustic percussion (congas, djembe, shakers, cowbells), electric guitar (for sampling and live looping).
